{"entities":[{"pmid":"17366349","text":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D009459","start":0,"end":30},{"pmid":"17366349","text":"ziprasidone","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"C092292","start":42,"end":53},{"pmid":"17366349","text":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D009459","start":86,"end":116},{"pmid":"17366349","text":"NMS","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D009459","start":118,"end":121},{"pmid":"17366349","text":"movement disorders","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D009069","start":181,"end":199},{"pmid":"17366349","text":"neuroleptic malignant syndrome","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D009459","start":223,"end":253},{"pmid":"17366349","text":"NMS","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D009459","start":255,"end":258},{"pmid":"17366349","text":"ziprasidone","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"C092292","start":287,"end":298},{"pmid":"17366349","text":"NMS","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D009459","start":371,"end":374},{"pmid":"17366349","text":"ziprasidone","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"C092292","start":410,"end":421},{"pmid":"17366349","text":"schizophrenia","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D012559","start":495,"end":508},{"pmid":"17366349","text":"NMS","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D009459","start":545,"end":548},{"pmid":"17366349","text":"ziprasidone","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"C092292","start":622,"end":633},{"pmid":"17366349","text":"NMS","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D009459","start":687,"end":690},{"pmid":"17366349","text":"ziprasidone","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"C092292","start":698,"end":709}],"gold_relations":"ziprasidone | CID | NMS","metrics":{"precision":0.5,"recall":1.0,"f1":0.6666666666666666,"tp":1,"fp":1,"fn":0,"rel_precision":0.5,"rel_recall":1.0,"rel_f1":0.6666666666666666,"rel_tp":1,"rel_fp":1,"rel_fn":0},"pmid":"17366349","relations":["C092292 | CID | D009459","C092292 | CID | D012559"],"text":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome induced by ziprasidone on the second day of treatment. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS) is the rarest and most serious of the neuroleptic-induced movement disorders. We describe a case of neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS) associated with the use of ziprasidone. Although conventional neuroleptics are more frequently associated with NMS, atypical antipsychotic drugs like ziprasidone may also be a cause. The patient is a 24-year-old male with a history of schizophrenia who developed signs and symptoms of NMS after 2 days of treatment with an 80-mg/day dose of orally administrated ziprasidone. This case is the earliest (second day of treatment) NMS due to ziprasidone reported in the literature."}